WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.281
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.581 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:18.456
Unit twenty-three: Food Systems. Today you will quantify claims using quantifiers (a great deal of / a significant proportion).

4
00:00:18.756 --> 00:00:21.501
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.328
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:27.266
Food system.

7
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:32.908
Food system means everything involved in producing, moving and consuming food.

8
00:00:33.517 --> 00:00:36.724
A food system links farmers, traders and consumers.

9
00:00:37.317 --> 00:00:40.480
Your turn. Say: Food system.

10
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:44.520
Food system.

11
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:46.517
Agriculture.

12
00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:51.002
Agriculture means the practice of farming crops and livestock.

13
00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:54.359
Agriculture employs most people in the region.

14
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.521
Your turn. Say: Agriculture.

15
00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:01.472
Agriculture.

16
00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:03.450
Supply chain.

17
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:08.667
Supply chain means the network that moves a product from producer to consumer.

18
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:11.736
A supply chain failure left shelves empty.

19
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.223
Your turn. Say: Supply chain.

20
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.683
Supply chain.

21
00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:21.453
Food waste.

22
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.390
Food waste means edible food that is thrown away.

23
00:01:25.983 --> 00:01:28.296
Food waste occurs at every stage.

24
00:01:28.883 --> 00:01:31.315
Your turn. Say: Food waste.

25
00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:35.330
Food waste.

26
00:01:36.083 --> 00:01:37.108
Nutrition.

27
00:01:38.117 --> 00:01:41.033
Nutrition means the intake of food needed for health.

28
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:45.275
Nutrition in childhood shapes lifelong health.

29
00:01:45.883 --> 00:01:48.570
Your turn. Say: Nutrition.

30
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:52.504
Nutrition.

31
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:54.507
Smallholder.

32
00:01:55.517 --> 00:01:58.967
Smallholder means a farmer who cultivates a small plot.

33
00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:02.256
Smallholders produce much of the world's food.

34
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.607
Your turn. Say: Smallholder.

35
00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:09.700
Smallholder.

36
00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:11.161
Climate.

37
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.345
Climate means the long-term pattern of weather in a region.

38
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.996
Climate change threatens harvests.

39
00:02:18.583 --> 00:02:21.038
Your turn. Say: Climate.

40
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:24.762
Climate.

41
00:02:25.517 --> 00:02:26.669
Security.

42
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:31.273
Security means the state of being reliably protected from lack.

43
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:35.160
Food security means reliable access to enough food.

44
00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.449
Your turn. Say: Security.

45
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.464
Security.

46
00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:46.755
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.617 --> 00:02:52.266
Visal, restaurants throw away so much food. How big is the problem really?

48
00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:59.634
A significant proportion of food is wasted, in some estimates nearly a third.

49
00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.030
Where does it happen?

50
00:03:03.117 --> 00:03:13.417
A great deal of waste occurs on farms and in storage in poorer countries, whereas a large amount is thrown out by households in richer ones.

51
00:03:14.517 --> 00:03:15.738
What can be done?

52
00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:29.522
A considerable number of smallholders lack cold storage. Investing in storage would save a substantial quantity of crops, and a small number of simple habits cut household waste.

53
00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:32.364
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:33.217 --> 00:03:38.026
Grammar focus: Quantifiers (a great deal of and a significant proportion).

55
00:03:38.326 --> 00:03:39.455
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.776
A significant proportion of food is wasted.

57
00:03:43.683 --> 00:03:46.112
A great deal of waste occurs on farms.

58
00:03:47.017 --> 00:03:50.293
A considerable number of smallholders lack cold storage.

59
00:03:51.233 --> 00:04:02.059
A great deal of / a large amount of / a significant proportion of + uncountable (food, waste); a large number of / a considerable number of + plural countable (farmers). The verb agrees with the noun.

60
00:04:02.817 --> 00:04:06.769
Be careful! Don't say: A great deal of foods is wasted.

61
00:04:08.733 --> 00:04:11.546
Say: A great deal of food is wasted.

62
00:04:11.846 --> 00:04:13.961
a great deal of + uncountable.

63
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:17.245
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:18.033 --> 00:04:19.301
From Farm to Fork.

65
00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:25.072
The global food system feeds billions, yet it wastes a significant proportion of what it produces.

66
00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:34.266
A great deal of food is lost on farms and in transport in poorer countries, whereas a large amount is discarded by households and retailers in richer ones.

67
00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:43.152
A considerable number of smallholders lack storage or roads, so a substantial quantity of their crops spoils before it reaches markets.

68
00:04:44.167 --> 00:04:50.369
Reducing waste would improve nutrition, save money and cut the emissions caused by producing uneaten food.

69
00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:58.405
A small number of practical measures, such as better storage, clearer date labels and meal planning, would make a major difference.

70
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.943
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:04.783 --> 00:05:07.665
A significant proportion of food blank wasted.

72
00:05:07.965 --> 00:05:11.038
A, is. B, are. C, were being.

73
00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.274
The answer is A: is.

74
00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:19.846
A great deal of blank occurs on farms.

75
00:05:20.146 --> 00:05:23.521
A, waste. B, wastes. C, wasting.

76
00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:28.892
The answer is A: waste.

77
00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.955
A farmer who cultivates a small plot is a blank.

78
00:05:33.255 --> 00:05:36.874
A, smallholder. B, supplier. C, trader.

79
00:05:40.433 --> 00:05:42.793
The answer is A: smallholder.

80
00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:45.671
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:45.971 --> 00:05:54.559
A, A large number of farmer lack storage.. B, A large number of farmers lack storage.. C, A large number of farmers lacks storage..

82
00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:01.876
The answer is B: A large number of farmers lack storage..

83
00:06:03.017 --> 00:06:07.292
According to the reading, reducing waste would improve nutrition, save money and cut…

84
00:06:07.592 --> 00:06:11.779
A, emissions. B, harvests. C, variety.

85
00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:17.553
The answer is A: emissions.

86
00:06:18.767 --> 00:06:22.046
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:22.883 --> 00:06:24.778
How can food waste be reduced?

88
00:06:41.583 --> 00:06:43.095
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:43.395 --> 00:07:09.844
A great deal of food waste can be reduced with simple measures. Farmers need better storage and transport so crops do not spoil. Shops can sell imperfect produce instead of discarding it. At home, a significant proportion of waste could be avoided by planning meals, storing food properly and using leftovers. Awareness matters as much as technology.

90
00:07:10.144 --> 00:07:11.865
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:18.502
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and quantifiers (a great deal of and a significant proportion).

92
00:07:18.802 --> 00:07:23.718
Today's value is food responsibility. Value food and the people who produce it.

93
00:07:24.018 --> 00:07:26.996
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.296 --> 00:07:30.122
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

